  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Shilluk\"\nsubtitle: \"South Sudan\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Shilluk, a Luo Nilotic people and the third-largest ethnic group in South Sudan |\n| Where | Along the western bank of the White Nile in the Upper Nile state of northeastern South Sudan |\n| How many | Not specified in the sources |\n| Language | Dhøg Cøllø, a Western Nilotic language with a rich tone system and irregular noun number marking |\n| Religion | Mostly Christian; some practice traditional religion or Islam |\n| Known for | The kingdom founded in 1454 with a divine royal lineage from the culture hero Nyikang · One of the earliest and fullest published bodies of African oral literature · Distinctive shell-covered headwear that marks rank and role · Agriculture and fishing more prominent than cattle-raising for a Nilotic people · Complex riddle and proverb traditions |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Shilluk are a Luo people who settled along the White Nile in South Sudan and founded a kingdom in 1454 ruled by a divine royal lineage. They are sedentary farmers and fishers who also raise cattle, and both men and women work the fields. Their culture is documented in one of the earliest and most complete published collections of African oral literature, collected in the early twentieth century.\n\n## Material culture\n\nShilluk material culture is known for shell-covered headwear marking rank, incised pottery, ironwork, and carved wooden objects, with pattern carried by pottery and shells rather than woven textiles.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nNo woven or dyed textile tradition is documented; pattern appears on pottery and shell-covered headwear instead.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nDocumented dress is primarily headwear that marks rank and role, made with shells, hair, and skin.\n\n- **Chief's head-dress** — Made of giraffe mane hair strung with cowrie and conus shells.\n- **Warrior's head-dress** — A circlet of skin carrying two rows of white cowrie shells with a fringe of black human hair.\n- **Rush-based head-dress** — Built over a base structure of rush stitched with rows of cowries, with two leather thongs for fastening and a body of human hair.\n- **Head-ring or crest** — Made of waterbuck hair, its exact use uncertain in the record.\n- **Arm-dagger** — A weapon with a leather and snake-skin sheath fitted with a loop to wear against the arm.\n\n### Architecture\n\nShilluk dwellings are round structures with conical thatched roofs, arranged in fenced compounds that contain both people and cattle.\n\n- **Round shelter** — A round structure with a conical thatched roof, documented in photographs from a village near Fashoda.\n- **Compound settlement** — Thatched shelters grouped together with cattle byres and enclosed by a fence of wood or basketry.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nPotters made incised vessels and animal-shaped pipes, while metalworkers produced dagger blades and carvers made wooden stools, clubs and shields.\n\n- **Hyena figure** — A pottery figure glazed dark brown with a white-filled dot pattern, also made as pipe-bowl shapes.\n- **Smoking pipe** — A composite object combining a pottery bowl, a stem of bamboo or carved wood, a gourd mouthpiece and hide stitching.\n- **Oval stool** — Carved from a solid block of wood on four feet.\n- **Giraffe-scapula spade** — A tool made from a giraffe shoulder blade and used for digging wells.\n- **Hide shield** — Made of giraffe, buffalo or hippopotamus hide with a wooden handle.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nIvory, beads, shells and human hair are worked into ornaments, particularly in elaborate headwear that signals social rank.\n\n- **Ivory armlet** — Armlets carved from ivory, worn on the upper arm.\n- **Stone disc bead necklace** — Forty-two thick stone disc beads, possibly quartz, threaded on vegetable-fibre cord with an elaborate fibre hook and loop closure and amber-coloured ring beads at each end.\n- **Glass bead bracelet** — A manufacture sample combining string, glass beads and metal.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nNo musical instruments or genres are named; songs were recorded in Shilluk with musical transcriptions in the early twentieth century.\n\n- **Dance staff** — A hand-held object used in dance, held in the British Museum but described no further.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nNo named dances, masks or theatrical forms are documented, though a symbolic mock battle is performed during weddings.\n\n- **Wedding mock battle** — A choreographed symbolic mock battle fought between the two families during a Shilluk wedding ceremony.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nMarriage ceremonies organised around bride-wealth transfer are the most fully documented rite, with fixed sequences of cattle transfer, feasting, mock battle and compound movement.\n\n- **Bride-wealth payment** (*diek nom*) — The ideal payment of roughly ten head of cattle together with several sheep, plus symbolic items including spears (jam nom), negotiable against what the groom could raise.\n- **Wedding ceremony sequence** — Fixed order: cattle are handed over, a wedding feast is held at the bride's home, families fight a symbolic mock battle, and the couple moves to the groom's family compound.\n- **First-birth custom** — The bride commonly returns to her natal home to give birth to her first child, affirming her lineage ties.\n- **Reth** — The divine royal ruler descended from Nyikang, whose health was understood to affect the health of the nation.\n\n## Foodways\n\nNo crops, dishes, cooking methods or food and drink traditions are documented; only the wedding feast at the bride's home is mentioned.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nShilluk oral literature was documented unusually early and fully in two major early twentieth-century collections containing tales, riddles, proverbs, songs and prayers.\n\n- **Riddle tradition** — Riddles describe a familiar thing as a scene while withholding its name; examples include 'Brothers who never hurt each other' (cow horns), 'the black-white cow is making white the earth' (moon), and 'a long row of trees full of white birds' (teeth).\n- **Proverb collection** — Over two hundred Shilluk proverbs recorded by Wilhelm Hofmayr with German translations.\n- **Nyikang** — The culture hero from whose lineage the reth, the divine ruler, descended.\n- **Westermann's 1912 collection** — Diedrich Westermann's *The Shilluk People, their Language and Folklore* containing sixty-one tales in Shilluk with English translations, prayers, songs, riddles and a grammar.\n- **Hofmayr's collection** — Wilhelm Hofmayr's *Die Schilluk* with over two hundred proverbs and riddles in German translation, plus songs with musical transcription.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nDhøg Cøllø is a Western Nilotic language with a rich tone system and famously irregular noun number marking; most Shilluk are Christian, some practice traditional religion or Islam.\n\n- **Dhøg Cøllø** — The Shilluk language, whose name means 'language' or 'mouth' (dhøg), belonging to the Luo branch of Western Nilotic with three level tones and four contour tones.\n- **Tone system** — Three level tonemes (Low, Mid, High) and four contours (Rise, Fall, High Fall, Late Fall).\n- **Advanced versus retracted tongue root** — The language distinguishes vowels by this feature, as most Nilotic languages do.\n- **Noun number marking** — Over 140 distinct patterns have been recorded with no predictable system identified.\n- **Latin alphabet** — Christian missionaries developed a 29-character Latin alphabet with 10 vowels and 19 consonants in the early twentieth century.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Dhøg Cøllø* — The Shilluk language; dhøg means both 'language' and 'mouth'\n- *diek nom* — Bride-wealth: roughly ten head of cattle and several sheep\n- *jam nom* — Symbolic spears included in bride-wealth payment\n- *reth* — The divine royal ruler descended from Nyikang\n- *Nyikang* — Culture hero from whose lineage the reth descended\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Diedrich Westermann, *The Shilluk People, their Language and Folklore*, 1912 — 61 tales with English translations, plus prayers, songs, riddles and a grammar\n- Wilhelm Hofmayr, *Die Schilluk. Geschichte, Religion und Leben eines Niloten-stammes* — over 200 proverbs and riddles with German translations, and songs with musical transcription\n- Greville G. Corbett, *Numbers*, Cambridge University Press, 2000, pp. 156–158 — on the number system of Dhok-Chollo\n- D. F. Robers, anthropometric survey of Nilotic stature, 1953–1954\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shilluk_people\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shilluk_language\n- Wikimedia Commons, media related to Shilluk\n- Library of Congress, photograph of two Shilluk men, 1936\n- British Museum online collection — the Shilluk arm-daggers, hide shields, giraffe-scapula spade, pottery pipes and hyena figures, shell head-dresses, ivory armlets and village photographs described above\n- https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=South+Sudan\n\n",
